{"parentUuid":"8b6b1994-3c4d-4c31-b2c7-06ac12de6300","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdpuwJGjvLvyQ1zbKdxoG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Good question, and the answer reframes the corpus problem.\n\n## NOSIBLE's HuggingFace has no retrieval corpus\n\nConfirmed live — the org is 8 repos, unchanged since Dec 2025: 5 Qwen3-0.6B classifiers and **3 datasets** (`financial-sentiment`, `forward-looking`, `prediction`). Those are 100k rows each, schema `text / label / netloc / url`, English, ODC-By. They're **classification training data**, not a searchable corpus — no queries, no qrels, no document IDs. They serve guide §3 (the classifier heads), not the 8-stage retrieval pipeline.\n\nThey're still valuable, and uniquely so: the text is drawn from NOSIBLE's own Search Feeds, already smol-html-cleaned and chunked to their 128–1024-token window. That's the only publicly available sample of their actual document distribution. Just not a corpus you can search.\n\n## The real constraint: shards\n\nThe blog's architecture is a federation of 250,000 shards, and stages 5–7 — shard selection, shard reranking, shard-based question expansion — exist *only* because the shards do. NOSIBLE calls shard selection \"arguably the single most important stage.\" With SciFact's 5,183 documents you get roughly one shard, which means the three stages the blog cares most about can't be exercised at all. **That's the fidelity argument for scale**, and it's much stronger than a bragging-rights argument.\n\nOne thing to flag: the blog's own numbers don't reconcile. \"Almost a billion webpages\" across 250,000 shards is ~4,000 docs/shard, but the worked example (shard 11,343) reports 134,523 documents — a 34× gap. Either the shard count is aspirational, or \"documents\" are chunks rather than pages. We'd have to pick an assumption and document it, which is exactly what the repo's Assumptions docstring header is for.\n\nEither way, to make shard *selection* a real decision you want ≥200 shards, so the target is roughly **1M–30M documents** depending on which shard-size reading we adopt.\n\n## Candidates\n\n| Corpus | Scale | Langs | Qrels | Licence | Fit |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| **FineWeb** (`sample-10BT`) | ~10B tokens | en | ✗ | ODC-By | Cleaned CommonCrawl — closest to NOSIBLE's actual input |\n| **FineWeb-2** | 1B–10B docs, 1000+ langs | multi | ✗ | ODC-By | Same, plus the Japanese the blog's example needs |\n| **MIRACL corpus** | 10M–100M passages, 16 langs | multi | ✓ expert | Apache-2.0 | Only multilingual retrieval eval at this scale |\n| **MS MARCO** | 1M–10M | en | ✓ | MS research-only | Classic IR eval; licence restricts use |\n| BEIR SciFact | 5,183 | en | ✓ | CC BY-SA | Current choice — ~1 shard, can't test stages 5–7 |\n| NOSIBLE ×3 | 300k rows | en | ✗ | ODC-By | Classifier heads only |\n\nMy recommendation is a **spine + eval split**: FineWeb-2 as the bulk corpus (gives you real shard structure, is ODC-By, and is cleaned-CommonCrawl like NOSIBLE's own input), with MIRACL folded in as the evaluation substrate — it carries real expert qrels *and* Japanese, so the blog's exact bilingual gene-editing example becomes runnable end to end. NOSIBLE's three datasets then get used for what they actually are: the classifier layer.\n\n**Before the machine question bites:** this container has **7.7 GiB RAM and 8 cores** (355G disk is fine). That caps you around 1–2M documents with int8-quantised embeddings. \"Single big machine\" needs to mean something bigger than what I'm running in — what are the specs of the box you're targeting, and is it this Mac or something else?"}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21143,"cache_read_input_tokens":80225,"output_tokens":3752,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21143,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3752,"cache_read_input_tokens":80225,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21143,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21143},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdpuwDrq5oW5wWwCMXXho","attributionMcpServer":"huggingface","attributionMcpTool":"hub_repo_details","type":"assistant","uuid":"f1691202-15e4-46d1-a5bd-55fde9ca2406","timestamp":"2026-08-08T07:49:18.855Z","effort":"high","session_id":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/workspaces/cybernaut_mini","sessionId":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","version":"2.1.223","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"f1691202-15e4-46d1-a5bd-55fde9ca2406","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdpuwJGjvLvyQ1zbKdxoG","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Wist1uSJR5R8vc36NWZWkG","name":"AskUserQuestion","input":{"questions":[{"question":"Which corpus spine should we build on?","header":"Corpus","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"FineWeb-2 + MIRACL evals (Recommended)","description":"FineWeb-2 as bulk corpus for real shard structure and multilingual coverage; MIRACL as the eval substrate for real qrels including Japanese. Both permissively licensed. Highest fidelity to NOSIBLE's cleaned-CommonCrawl input."},{"label":"MIRACL only","description":"Simpler: one dataset, 16 languages, real qrels throughout, 10M-100M passages. Wikipedia-derived so less web-like than NOSIBLE's actual distribution, but every document is eval-backed."},{"label":"Raw CommonCrawl + smol-html","description":"Maximum fidelity: crawl WARC files and clean them with NOSIBLE's own smol-html tool, reproducing their ingest exactly. Much more work, no qrels at all, and heavy on bandwidth and CPU."},{"label":"Keep SciFact, scale later","description":"Leave the corpus decision alone for now and build the missing components at current size first. Defers the scale question entirely."}]},{"question":"Where do relevance judgments come from at scale?","header":"Evals","multiSelect":false,"options":[{"label":"Real qrels only","description":"Only evaluate on documents that carry human/expert judgments (MIRACL, BEIR). Strictest reading of your no-dummy-data rule, but limits eval coverage to a fraction of the corpus."},{"label":"NOSIBLE-style generated per-shard evals","description":"Reproduce what the blog actually describes: each shard carries its own LLM-generated gold-standard eval set, over real documents. Queries are synthetic, documents are real. This is what NOSIBLE does."},{"label":"Both, clearly separated","description":"Real qrels as the trusted benchmark; generated per-shard evals as the fidelity reproduction, kept in separate namespaces so results are never conflated."}]}]},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21143,"cache_read_input_tokens":80225,"output_tokens":3752,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21143,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":1,"output_tokens":3752,"cache_read_input_tokens":80225,"cache_creation_input_tokens":21143,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":21143},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"diagnostics":null},"requestId":"req_011CdpuwDrq5oW5wWwCMXXho","attributionMcpServer":"huggingface","attributionMcpTool":"hub_repo_details","type":"assistant","uuid":"14af2414-fe84-46a3-a446-1ccc67bb5b56","timestamp":"2026-08-08T07:49:27.845Z","effort":"high","session_id":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/workspaces/cybernaut_mini","sessionId":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","version":"2.1.223","gitBranch":"main"}
