{"parentUuid":"e70797a7-bd2d-48fa-ae3a-14a83c2a9015","isSidechain":false,"attachment":{"type":"hook_success","hookName":"PreToolUse:mcp__exa__web_search_exa","toolUseID":"toolu_01GpA67dSmmGf4uW9SY1rP5d","hookEvent":"PreToolUse","content":"","stdout":"{\"continue\":true,\"suppressOutput\":true}\n","stderr":"","exitCode":0,"command":"node \"$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT\"/scripts/run.cjs \"$CLAUDE_PLUGIN_ROOT\"/scripts/pre-tool-enforcer.mjs","durationMs":74},"type":"attachment","uuid":"4efd19df-96df-427d-9e4c-6fc6abcbe6aa","timestamp":"2026-08-08T07:54:56.824Z","session_id":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/workspaces/cybernaut_mini","sessionId":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","version":"2.1.223","gitBranch":"main"}
{"parentUuid":"e70797a7-bd2d-48fa-ae3a-14a83c2a9015","isSidechain":false,"promptId":"d90ae2a3-afc9-48af-9996-7f3197eaebf2","type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01GpA67dSmmGf4uW9SY1rP5d","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Title: Introducing Cybernaut-1: Agentic Search using MCTS - NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/blog/introducing-cybernaut-1-agentic-search-with-mcts\nPublished: 2025-08-26T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Stuart Reid\nHighlights:\nToday I am proud to announce the release of Cybernaut-1. Cybernaut-\n...\ncombines our powerful hybrid-3 search algorithm with LLM-guided Monte Carlo Tree Search to deliver world class search results on difficult queries. Cybernaut-1 is available via our V2 Search API and our Python Package.\n...\nNext week, we will be open-sourcing a comprehensive set of evaluations showing how Cybernaut-1 as well as Hybrid-3, the algorithm it uses, consistently match or outperforms leading search engines, even as we continue expanding our web coverage (currently growing at ~20 million webpages per day).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Search API | NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/search-api\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nProduction SERP. Same hybrid-3 ranker, lifted caps, built for live application traffic.\n...\nCybernaut-1 writes the queries for you. Agentic SERP, ranked search results back.\n...\nFree, fast, bulk, and enriched: same request shape, four different cost / depth profiles. Pick the one that matches your traffic, scale up later without rewriting the call site.\n...\n- questionstring · required1–500 chars.\n- algorithmenum · hybrid-3Default workhorse ranker. Lexical, baseline, hamming, hybrid-1/2/3, and company are supported.\n- n_resultsint · 10–100 · 10Up to a hundred hits per call.\n- n_probes · filters · expansions · …—Full parameter list. See the OpenAPI spec.\n...\n- questionstring · requiredSame as the SERP endpoints.\n- algorithmenum · hybrid-3Same workhorse ranker as the live SERP endpoints.\n- n_resultsint · 1,000–10,000 · 1,000Long-tail window: up to ten thousand ranked results.\n- n_probes · filters · expansions · …—Full parameter list. See the OpenAPI spec.\n...\nAgentic SERP, not deep research. Cybernaut-1 plans multi-angle strategies, fans out parallel probes, and returns the same ranked search results as the SERP endpoints: the query is what the agent authored for you.\n...\n- promptstring · required25–2,500 chars. Describe what you need to know.\n- agentenum · cybernaut-1Currently only \"cybernaut-1\".\n...\nSame ranked-result shape as the SERP endpoints: the agent authored the queries, you read the results.\n...\nright query is\n...\nknown in advance. Let the agent author the queries and return the same ranked results your SERP code already consumes.\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Road to Cybernaut-1: Rebuilding Search for AI | NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/blog/the-road-to-cybernaut-1\nPublished: 2025-08-20T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Stuart Reid\nHighlights:\n25\n...\ncapable of performing lexical retrieval, semantic retrieval, full\n...\n, hybrid retrieval\n...\nmore complex tasks\n...\n- Evals - Gold standard datasets for evaluating this shard.\n...\ncalibrated lexical search indices beat well-calibrated semantic search indices on AI queries more often than not. Why\n...\nlonger and have\n...\nsearch dominates both\n...\nthat have placeholders for named entities, geographic regions, topics of interest, and other dimensions. In our internal\n...\nseen that optimizing the instruction - or getting a small LLM to write a bespoke one - can yield\n...\n1-5% improvement in search precision and recall.\n...\nAs I mentioned earlier - Human search engines must be idiot-proof; AI search engines must be the opposite: genius-friendly. In the coming days we will launch our V2 search API which includes an algorithm called cybernaut-1.\n...\nCybernaut-1 is an AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, reranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\nPut differently, cybernaut-1 is a reinforcement learner that designs and iterates on search policies to maximize search relevancy. It is the culmination of months of work, and it will be generally available on the 25th of August 2025.\n...\nThe relationship between cybernaut-1 and NOSIBLE is a high-trust one. We trust that cybernaut-1 is able to understand and navigate the complexity of our search engine, so we surface it instead of hiding it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Introducing Cybernaut-1: Agentic Search using MCTS\nURL: https://nosible.ghost.io/introducing-cybernaut-1-agentic-search-with-mcts/\nPublished: 2025-08-26T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nToday I am proud to announce the release of Cybernaut-1. Cybernaut-1 combines our powerful hybrid-3 search algorithm with LLM-guided Monte Carlo Tree Search to deliver world class search results on difficult queries. Cybernaut-1 is available via our V2 Search API and our Python Package.\n...\nNext week, we will be open-sourcing a comprehensive set of evaluations showing how Cybernaut-1 as well as Hybrid-3, the algorithm it uses, consistently match or outperforms leading search engines, even as we continue expanding our web coverage (currently growing at ~20 million webpages per day).\n\n---\n\nTitle: NosibleAI/nosible-py\nURL: https://github.com/NosibleAI/nosible-py\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n### 🤖 Cybernaut 1\n...\nAn AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, \nreranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\n```python\nfrom nosible import Nosible\n...\nwith Nosible(nosible_api_key=\"YOUR API KEY\") as client:\n\n    results = client.search(\n        # search() gives you access to Cybernaut 1\n        prompt=\"Find me interesting technical blogs about Monte Carlo Tree Search.\"\n    )\n\n    print(results)\n\n---\n\nTitle: NOSIBLE Search Client — Nosible Client 0.3.13 documentation\nURL: https://nosible-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## 🤖 Cybernaut 1[](#cybernaut-1 \"Link to this heading\")\n...\nAn AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, reranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\nfrom nosible import Nosible\n...\nwith Nosible(nosible_api_key=\"YOUR API KEY\") as client:\n\n results = client.search(\n # search() gives you access to Cybernaut 1\n prompt=\"Find me interesting technical blogs about Monte Carlo Tree Search.\"\n )\n\n print(results)\n...\nTo easily view examples of how to use the client package, or demos to see what can be achieved with the NOSIBLE search engine, view our [examples and demos page](https://nosible-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/examples.html).\n\n---\n\nTitle: nosible - npm\nURL: https://www.npmjs.com/package/nosible\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n0.2.21 • Public • Published 4 months ago\n...\nA high-level TypeScript/JavaScript client for the NOSIBLE Search API. Easily integrate the Nosible Search API into your TypeScript and JavaScript projects.\n...\n## ✨ Features\n...\n- 🚀 Fast Search - Quick searches optimized for speed (10-100 results)\n- 🤖 AI Search - Advanced AI-powered searches with custom instructions\n- 📦 Bulk Search - Large-scale data retrieval (1000-10000 results)\n- 🔄 Parallel Searches - Execute multiple searches concurrently\n- 🌐 URL Scraping - Extract structured content from web pages\n- 📈 Topic Trends - Analyze topic popularity over time\n- 🎯 Advanced Filtering - SQL filters, date ranges, geographic filters, and more\n- ✅ Type Safe - Full TypeScript support with IntelliSense\n- 🔒 Secure - API key authentication\n...\n// Search with advanced filtering options\n...\nfastSearch({\n...\nmachine learning startups\n...\nnResults: 50,\n...\nalgorithm: \"hybrid-2\",\n...\nminSimilarity:\n...\n0.7,\n  mustInclude: [\"artificial intelligence\", \"funding\"],\n  mustExclude: [\"cryptocurrency\"],\n  continent: \"North America\",\n  brandSafety\n...\n\"high\",\n...\n// Create a search with specific parameters\nconst search = new Search({\n  question: \"quantum computing breakthroughs\",\n  nResults: 30,\n  algorithm: \"hybrid-3\",\n  continent: \"Europe\",\n  publishStart: new Date(\"2024-01-01\"),\n  publishEnd: new Date(\"2025-01-01\"),\n});\n...\n// Execute the search\nconst results = await client.fastSearch({search});\n...\n```\n// Bulk search for comprehensive data gathering\nconst bulkResults = await client.bulkSearch({\n  question: \"all articles about artificial intelligence\",\n  nResults: 5000,\n  algorithm: \"hybrid-2\",\n  minSimilarity: 0.6,\n});\n\n---\n\nTitle: youdotcom-oss/web-search-api-evals\nURL: https://github.com/youdotcom-oss/web-search-api-evals\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThis repository contains evaluation framework for AI-first web search APIs. Each API is integrated as a sampler and evaluated across benchmarks that test accuracy, latency, and information retrieval performance.\n...\nThe framework supports multiple search providers (You.com, Exa, Tavily, Parallel) and a representative \nGoogle SERP–based sampler. For each query, search results are fetched from the search API, synthesized into an answer \nusing an LLM, then graded against the ground truth.[^1] It also includes a dedicated [finance evaluation](#finance-evaluation) suite for benchmarking financial data retrieval.\n...\nBelow are evaluation results across different search samplers and benchmark suites. Grading is performed via an LLM \njudge (GPT 5.4 mini) using prompts from the standard benchmarks (as specified in the original papers or repositories).[^2]\nGPT 5.4 nano was used as the synthesis model.\n...\n**SimpleQA**\n\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms* |\n|---------------------------|----------|-----------------|\n| you_search_with_livecrawl |**92.09%**| 1048.05 |\n| exa_search_with_text | 90.06% | 1176.05 |\n| parallel_search_basic | 89.78% | 1901.66 |\n| tavily_advanced | 86.32% | 3190.00 |\n| you_search | 84.81% | 538.44 |\n| google_search | 80.17% | 1347.48 |\n| tavily_basic | 59.11% | 1340.00 |\n...\n**FRAMES**\n...\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------|\n| you_research_lite | 70.75% | 3939.82 |\n| tavily_advanced | 39.93% | 3460.00 |\n| exa_search_with_text | 39.81% | 1351.75 |\n| you_search_with_livecrawl | 37.26% | 1153.78 |\n| parallel_search_basic | 34.83% | 2118.61 |\n| you_search | 28.03% | 565.80 |\n| google_search | 22.94% | 1475.05 |\n| tavily_basic | 19.30% | 2180.00 |\n...\n### Supported Benchmarks\n...\n| Benchmark | Description | Flag / usage |\n|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| SimpleQA | Factual question answering ([OpenAI SimpleQA](https://openai.com/index/introducing-simpleqa/)) | `--datasets simpleqa` |\n| FRAMES | Deep research and multi-hop reasoning ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12941), [dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/frames-benchmark)) | `--datasets frames` |\n| DeepSearchQA | Challenging multi-step information seeking tasks. Only recommended for use with research endpoints ([paper](https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepSearchQA/DeepSearchQA_benchmark_paper.pdf), [dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/deepsearchqa)) | `--datasets deepsearchqa` |\n...\n| BrowseComp | A simple and challenging benchmark that measures the ability of AI agents to locate hard-to-find information. Only recommended for use with research endpoints ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12516), [dataset](https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/simple-evals/browse_comp_test_set.csv)) | `--datasets browsecomp` |\n...\n| FinSearchComp T2 & T3 | Public-company financial lookup benchmarks from filings ([paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.13160)). T2 covers simple historical lookups; T3 covers complex historical investigations. Grading follows the paper's judge prompt; numbers in different formats (e.g. `12.45%` vs `0.1245`) are treated as equivalent. | `--datasets fin_search_comp_t2_global fin_search_comp_t3_global` |\n...\nThe `fin_search_comp_t2_global` dataset evaluates simple historical lookup of public-company financials (e.g. *\"What were Uber's research and development expenses for the full year 2019?\"*). Ground truth comes from SEC filings and grading follows the prompt from the FinSearchComp paper[^3], which treats numerically equivalent answers (`12.45%` vs `0.1245`, `120,400,000` vs `120.4 million`) as the same and ignores unit-only differences. The grader model is configurable independently of the default `GRADER_MODEL` via `FIN_SEARCH_GRADER_MODEL` in `src/evals/constants.py`.\n...\n### Samplers evaluated against this benchmark\n...\n| Sampler | Provider |\n|-------------------------------------------|------------|\n| `you_finance_research_deep` | You.com |\n| `you_finance_research_exhaustive` | You.com |\n| `perplexity_finance_historical_lookup` | Perplexity |\n| `perplexity_finance_multi_step_research` | Perplexity |\n| `perplexity_sonar_deep_research_high` | Perplexity |\n| `exa_research_pro` | Exa |\n| `tavily_research_pro` | Tavily |\n| `parallel_pro` | Parallel |\n| `parallel_ultra` | Parallel |\n...\n**FinSearchComp T2 — Simple historical lookup (global)**\n\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms* |\n|------------------------------------------|------------|-----------------|\n| you_finance_research_deep | **87.29%** | 124.0 |\n| parallel_ultra | 73.11% | 861.3 |\n| perplexity_finance_historical_lookup | 72.27% | 32.2 |\n| perplexity_sonar_deep_research_high | 53.78% | 92.6 |\n| exa_research_pro | 42.02% | 366.8 |\n| tavily_research_pro | 40.34% | 104.5 |\n| parallel_pro | 34.45% | 317.0 |"}]}]},"uuid":"ff228d01-7729-4848-996f-7b4b1e1d976b","timestamp":"2026-08-08T07:54:58.293Z","toolUseResult":[{"type":"text","text":"Title: Introducing Cybernaut-1: Agentic Search using MCTS - NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/blog/introducing-cybernaut-1-agentic-search-with-mcts\nPublished: 2025-08-26T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Stuart Reid\nHighlights:\nToday I am proud to announce the release of Cybernaut-1. Cybernaut-\n...\ncombines our powerful hybrid-3 search algorithm with LLM-guided Monte Carlo Tree Search to deliver world class search results on difficult queries. Cybernaut-1 is available via our V2 Search API and our Python Package.\n...\nNext week, we will be open-sourcing a comprehensive set of evaluations showing how Cybernaut-1 as well as Hybrid-3, the algorithm it uses, consistently match or outperforms leading search engines, even as we continue expanding our web coverage (currently growing at ~20 million webpages per day).\n\n---\n\nTitle: Search API | NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/search-api\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nProduction SERP. Same hybrid-3 ranker, lifted caps, built for live application traffic.\n...\nCybernaut-1 writes the queries for you. Agentic SERP, ranked search results back.\n...\nFree, fast, bulk, and enriched: same request shape, four different cost / depth profiles. Pick the one that matches your traffic, scale up later without rewriting the call site.\n...\n- questionstring · required1–500 chars.\n- algorithmenum · hybrid-3Default workhorse ranker. Lexical, baseline, hamming, hybrid-1/2/3, and company are supported.\n- n_resultsint · 10–100 · 10Up to a hundred hits per call.\n- n_probes · filters · expansions · …—Full parameter list. See the OpenAPI spec.\n...\n- questionstring · requiredSame as the SERP endpoints.\n- algorithmenum · hybrid-3Same workhorse ranker as the live SERP endpoints.\n- n_resultsint · 1,000–10,000 · 1,000Long-tail window: up to ten thousand ranked results.\n- n_probes · filters · expansions · …—Full parameter list. See the OpenAPI spec.\n...\nAgentic SERP, not deep research. Cybernaut-1 plans multi-angle strategies, fans out parallel probes, and returns the same ranked search results as the SERP endpoints: the query is what the agent authored for you.\n...\n- promptstring · required25–2,500 chars. Describe what you need to know.\n- agentenum · cybernaut-1Currently only \"cybernaut-1\".\n...\nSame ranked-result shape as the SERP endpoints: the agent authored the queries, you read the results.\n...\nright query is\n...\nknown in advance. Let the agent author the queries and return the same ranked results your SERP code already consumes.\n\n---\n\nTitle: The Road to Cybernaut-1: Rebuilding Search for AI | NOSIBLE\nURL: https://nosible.com/blog/the-road-to-cybernaut-1\nPublished: 2025-08-20T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: Stuart Reid\nHighlights:\n25\n...\ncapable of performing lexical retrieval, semantic retrieval, full\n...\n, hybrid retrieval\n...\nmore complex tasks\n...\n- Evals - Gold standard datasets for evaluating this shard.\n...\ncalibrated lexical search indices beat well-calibrated semantic search indices on AI queries more often than not. Why\n...\nlonger and have\n...\nsearch dominates both\n...\nthat have placeholders for named entities, geographic regions, topics of interest, and other dimensions. In our internal\n...\nseen that optimizing the instruction - or getting a small LLM to write a bespoke one - can yield\n...\n1-5% improvement in search precision and recall.\n...\nAs I mentioned earlier - Human search engines must be idiot-proof; AI search engines must be the opposite: genius-friendly. In the coming days we will launch our V2 search API which includes an algorithm called cybernaut-1.\n...\nCybernaut-1 is an AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, reranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\nPut differently, cybernaut-1 is a reinforcement learner that designs and iterates on search policies to maximize search relevancy. It is the culmination of months of work, and it will be generally available on the 25th of August 2025.\n...\nThe relationship between cybernaut-1 and NOSIBLE is a high-trust one. We trust that cybernaut-1 is able to understand and navigate the complexity of our search engine, so we surface it instead of hiding it.\n\n---\n\nTitle: Introducing Cybernaut-1: Agentic Search using MCTS\nURL: https://nosible.ghost.io/introducing-cybernaut-1-agentic-search-with-mcts/\nPublished: 2025-08-26T00:00:00.000Z\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nToday I am proud to announce the release of Cybernaut-1. Cybernaut-1 combines our powerful hybrid-3 search algorithm with LLM-guided Monte Carlo Tree Search to deliver world class search results on difficult queries. Cybernaut-1 is available via our V2 Search API and our Python Package.\n...\nNext week, we will be open-sourcing a comprehensive set of evaluations showing how Cybernaut-1 as well as Hybrid-3, the algorithm it uses, consistently match or outperforms leading search engines, even as we continue expanding our web coverage (currently growing at ~20 million webpages per day).\n\n---\n\nTitle: NosibleAI/nosible-py\nURL: https://github.com/NosibleAI/nosible-py\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n### 🤖 Cybernaut 1\n...\nAn AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, \nreranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\n```python\nfrom nosible import Nosible\n...\nwith Nosible(nosible_api_key=\"YOUR API KEY\") as client:\n\n    results = client.search(\n        # search() gives you access to Cybernaut 1\n        prompt=\"Find me interesting technical blogs about Monte Carlo Tree Search.\"\n    )\n\n    print(results)\n\n---\n\nTitle: NOSIBLE Search Client — Nosible Client 0.3.13 documentation\nURL: https://nosible-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n## 🤖 Cybernaut 1[](#cybernaut-1 \"Link to this heading\")\n...\nAn AI agent with unrestricted access to everything in NOSIBLE including every shard, algorithm, selector, reranker, and signal. It knows what these things are and can tune them on the fly to find better results.\n...\nfrom nosible import Nosible\n...\nwith Nosible(nosible_api_key=\"YOUR API KEY\") as client:\n\n results = client.search(\n # search() gives you access to Cybernaut 1\n prompt=\"Find me interesting technical blogs about Monte Carlo Tree Search.\"\n )\n\n print(results)\n...\nTo easily view examples of how to use the client package, or demos to see what can be achieved with the NOSIBLE search engine, view our [examples and demos page](https://nosible-py.readthedocs.io/en/latest/examples.html).\n\n---\n\nTitle: nosible - npm\nURL: https://www.npmjs.com/package/nosible\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\n0.2.21 • Public • Published 4 months ago\n...\nA high-level TypeScript/JavaScript client for the NOSIBLE Search API. Easily integrate the Nosible Search API into your TypeScript and JavaScript projects.\n...\n## ✨ Features\n...\n- 🚀 Fast Search - Quick searches optimized for speed (10-100 results)\n- 🤖 AI Search - Advanced AI-powered searches with custom instructions\n- 📦 Bulk Search - Large-scale data retrieval (1000-10000 results)\n- 🔄 Parallel Searches - Execute multiple searches concurrently\n- 🌐 URL Scraping - Extract structured content from web pages\n- 📈 Topic Trends - Analyze topic popularity over time\n- 🎯 Advanced Filtering - SQL filters, date ranges, geographic filters, and more\n- ✅ Type Safe - Full TypeScript support with IntelliSense\n- 🔒 Secure - API key authentication\n...\n// Search with advanced filtering options\n...\nfastSearch({\n...\nmachine learning startups\n...\nnResults: 50,\n...\nalgorithm: \"hybrid-2\",\n...\nminSimilarity:\n...\n0.7,\n  mustInclude: [\"artificial intelligence\", \"funding\"],\n  mustExclude: [\"cryptocurrency\"],\n  continent: \"North America\",\n  brandSafety\n...\n\"high\",\n...\n// Create a search with specific parameters\nconst search = new Search({\n  question: \"quantum computing breakthroughs\",\n  nResults: 30,\n  algorithm: \"hybrid-3\",\n  continent: \"Europe\",\n  publishStart: new Date(\"2024-01-01\"),\n  publishEnd: new Date(\"2025-01-01\"),\n});\n...\n// Execute the search\nconst results = await client.fastSearch({search});\n...\n```\n// Bulk search for comprehensive data gathering\nconst bulkResults = await client.bulkSearch({\n  question: \"all articles about artificial intelligence\",\n  nResults: 5000,\n  algorithm: \"hybrid-2\",\n  minSimilarity: 0.6,\n});\n\n---\n\nTitle: youdotcom-oss/web-search-api-evals\nURL: https://github.com/youdotcom-oss/web-search-api-evals\nPublished: N/A\nAuthor: N/A\nHighlights:\nThis repository contains evaluation framework for AI-first web search APIs. Each API is integrated as a sampler and evaluated across benchmarks that test accuracy, latency, and information retrieval performance.\n...\nThe framework supports multiple search providers (You.com, Exa, Tavily, Parallel) and a representative \nGoogle SERP–based sampler. For each query, search results are fetched from the search API, synthesized into an answer \nusing an LLM, then graded against the ground truth.[^1] It also includes a dedicated [finance evaluation](#finance-evaluation) suite for benchmarking financial data retrieval.\n...\nBelow are evaluation results across different search samplers and benchmark suites. Grading is performed via an LLM \njudge (GPT 5.4 mini) using prompts from the standard benchmarks (as specified in the original papers or repositories).[^2]\nGPT 5.4 nano was used as the synthesis model.\n...\n**SimpleQA**\n\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms* |\n|---------------------------|----------|-----------------|\n| you_search_with_livecrawl |**92.09%**| 1048.05 |\n| exa_search_with_text | 90.06% | 1176.05 |\n| parallel_search_basic | 89.78% | 1901.66 |\n| tavily_advanced | 86.32% | 3190.00 |\n| you_search | 84.81% | 538.44 |\n| google_search | 80.17% | 1347.48 |\n| tavily_basic | 59.11% | 1340.00 |\n...\n**FRAMES**\n...\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms |\n|---------------------------|----------|----------------|\n| you_research_lite | 70.75% | 3939.82 |\n| tavily_advanced | 39.93% | 3460.00 |\n| exa_search_with_text | 39.81% | 1351.75 |\n| you_search_with_livecrawl | 37.26% | 1153.78 |\n| parallel_search_basic | 34.83% | 2118.61 |\n| you_search | 28.03% | 565.80 |\n| google_search | 22.94% | 1475.05 |\n| tavily_basic | 19.30% | 2180.00 |\n...\n### Supported Benchmarks\n...\n| Benchmark | Description | Flag / usage |\n|--------------|-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|---------------------------|\n| SimpleQA | Factual question answering ([OpenAI SimpleQA](https://openai.com/index/introducing-simpleqa/)) | `--datasets simpleqa` |\n| FRAMES | Deep research and multi-hop reasoning ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.12941), [dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/frames-benchmark)) | `--datasets frames` |\n| DeepSearchQA | Challenging multi-step information seeking tasks. Only recommended for use with research endpoints ([paper](https://storage.googleapis.com/deepmind-media/DeepSearchQA/DeepSearchQA_benchmark_paper.pdf), [dataset](https://huggingface.co/datasets/google/deepsearchqa)) | `--datasets deepsearchqa` |\n...\n| BrowseComp | A simple and challenging benchmark that measures the ability of AI agents to locate hard-to-find information. Only recommended for use with research endpoints ([paper](https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.12516), [dataset](https://openaipublic.blob.core.windows.net/simple-evals/browse_comp_test_set.csv)) | `--datasets browsecomp` |\n...\n| FinSearchComp T2 & T3 | Public-company financial lookup benchmarks from filings ([paper](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2509.13160)). T2 covers simple historical lookups; T3 covers complex historical investigations. Grading follows the paper's judge prompt; numbers in different formats (e.g. `12.45%` vs `0.1245`) are treated as equivalent. | `--datasets fin_search_comp_t2_global fin_search_comp_t3_global` |\n...\nThe `fin_search_comp_t2_global` dataset evaluates simple historical lookup of public-company financials (e.g. *\"What were Uber's research and development expenses for the full year 2019?\"*). Ground truth comes from SEC filings and grading follows the prompt from the FinSearchComp paper[^3], which treats numerically equivalent answers (`12.45%` vs `0.1245`, `120,400,000` vs `120.4 million`) as the same and ignores unit-only differences. The grader model is configurable independently of the default `GRADER_MODEL` via `FIN_SEARCH_GRADER_MODEL` in `src/evals/constants.py`.\n...\n### Samplers evaluated against this benchmark\n...\n| Sampler | Provider |\n|-------------------------------------------|------------|\n| `you_finance_research_deep` | You.com |\n| `you_finance_research_exhaustive` | You.com |\n| `perplexity_finance_historical_lookup` | Perplexity |\n| `perplexity_finance_multi_step_research` | Perplexity |\n| `perplexity_sonar_deep_research_high` | Perplexity |\n| `exa_research_pro` | Exa |\n| `tavily_research_pro` | Tavily |\n| `parallel_pro` | Parallel |\n| `parallel_ultra` | Parallel |\n...\n**FinSearchComp T2 — Simple historical lookup (global)**\n\n| sampler | accuracy | p50_latency_ms* |\n|------------------------------------------|------------|-----------------|\n| you_finance_research_deep | **87.29%** | 124.0 |\n| parallel_ultra | 73.11% | 861.3 |\n| perplexity_finance_historical_lookup | 72.27% | 32.2 |\n| perplexity_sonar_deep_research_high | 53.78% | 92.6 |\n| exa_research_pro | 42.02% | 366.8 |\n| tavily_research_pro | 40.34% | 104.5 |\n| parallel_pro | 34.45% | 317.0 |","_meta":{"searchTime":1035.9}}],"sourceToolAssistantUUID":"e70797a7-bd2d-48fa-ae3a-14a83c2a9015","session_id":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/workspaces/cybernaut_mini","sessionId":"1739850f-85bf-4d10-b02b-a0b2492e7df8","version":"2.1.223","gitBranch":"main"}
