{"parentUuid":"50255d89-0ff5-4c95-bc62-60ae51772b99","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CdpvV4DnijiyqEiQN4kg2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Recorded in the decisions wiki, including the correction rather than a quiet overwrite.\n\n**Net effect of the cross-check:** the blocking question dissolved. I'd been treating \"single big machine\" as a prerequisite; at the real 1,000 docs/shard ratio, a 200k–1M document corpus gives genuine shard structure and runs on what's already here. The machine spec now only matters past ~1M documents, which is a later problem.\n\nThat also settles the sequencing question by itself. Components-first was my recommendation on the argument that scaling an incomplete stack means scaling it twice — and now there's no reason to scale first, since the corpus that makes stages 5–7 testable is small enough to build against immediately.\n\nSo the concrete next step I'd propose: pull a **FineWeb-2 slice sized to ~200 shards (~200k docs)** plus the **MIRACL Japanese slice with its qrels**, replace SciFact in the catalog, and get the shard-learning step running — because until shards exist, stages 5–7 have nothing to select from, and those are the stages the whole architecture is about. Everything else on the NOT-BUILT list (bloom filters, zstd dictionaries, Aho-Corasick, the Bayesian selector, PPR reranker) attaches to shards once they do.\n\nSay the word and I'll plan that out properly with the docs/notebook obligations attached. Two things still genuinely need your call, and I won't guess on either:\n\n- **`e149331`** (gstack submodule) — park it like the trade shard, or keep it?\n- The three uncommitted `.devcontainer/` edits — commit them?"}],"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3948,"cache_read_input_tokens":132623,"output_tokens":673,"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch